  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Tuesday) \nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nMuch better precipitation chances will arrive along and behind \nthe cold front Wednesday evening and into Wednesday night as the \nfront pushes into northeast OK, and eventually into far northwest\nAR by Thursday morning. A limited severe storm threat will exist \nnear the front, at least initially. The highest threat will lie \nnorth of I-44, where an unstable, hot and humid airmass may help \ncause strong to severe downburst wind gusts, small hail, and \nlocally heavy rain with more robust storms. As the sun sets and \nthe boundary layer nocturnally stabilizes, the severe threat is \nexpected to lower by mid-late evening Wednesday. \n\nModel guidance continues to suggest the front will slow down and \nstall in the vicinity or just south of I-40 by sunrise Thursday \nmorning. Scattered showers and thunderstorms will linger through \nthe daytime Thursday with the front remaining in the area and the \nmid-level trough axis overhead. Behind the front, temperatures \nwill fight hard to get out of the 80s. Ahead of the frontal \nboundary, unseasonably hot temperatures will persevere, though \nrain chances and cloud cover should help keep high temperatures in\nthe mid-upper 90s, and not reach triple digits. It is possible \nlow-end Heat Advisory criteria may briefly be met for a few areas\nsouth of I-40. \n\nThe frontal boundary will lift northward Thursday\nafternoon/evening and southerly winds will prevail by Friday\nmorning. Widely scattered showers and thunderstorm will linger\nthrough the morning and perhaps the afternoon as the front lifts\nout of the area. Precipitation amounts are expected to stay\nrelatively light overall. \n\nGlobal models continue to show another cold front approaching from\nthe north on Saturday, with disagreements on timing and how far\nsouth it is able to drift. The front will bring another shot of \nshowers and thunderstorms, but highest chances will be focused in \nits vicinity, and therefore much of the precipitation chances \nassociated with the boundary will be mostly confined north of I-40\nthrough Saturday night. Despite the front and weaker ridging aloft,\ntemperatures are expected to remain a handful of degrees above \naverage through the upcoming weekend and into early next week. \nMedium-long range NPW models further diverge with their solutions \nbeyond Sunday. But consensus with them seems to indicate a \nstronger cold front approaching the area beyond the long-term \nperiod. \n\nMejia",
